\exfigure{335_q28_s1}

$$\begin{aligned} \int_0^1\sqrt{1-x^2}\,dx&=\text{area of quadrant}\\ &=\dfrac{1}{4}\times\pi\times 1^2\\ &=\dfrac{\pi}{4} \end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned} \dfrac{\pi}{4}&\approx 0.748927\ldots\\ \pi&\approx 2.99570\ldots\\ &\approx 2.996 \end{aligned}$$

\exfigure{335_q32_s1}

The wire forms two radii and the arc $l=r\theta$:

$$\begin{aligned} L&=2r+r\theta\\ r\theta&=L-2r\\ \theta&=\dfrac{L-2r}{r} \end{aligned}$$

\exfigure{335_q35_s1}

The mode occurs at the maximum value on the graph and the median occurs at the point which divides the area under the curve in half.

From the graph we can see the area to the left of the mode is clearly greater than half the total area. Thus, the median must be to the left of the mode i.e. mode > median.
